WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.031
In 2012, Facebook faced a serious engineering problem.

2
00:00:04.031 --> 00:00:07.987
The consumer internet was shifting to mobile, but the

3
00:00:07.987 --> 00:00:11.346
hardware landscape was incredibly fragmented.

4
00:00:11.346 --> 00:00:15.900
Building entirely separate native code bases for thousands of

5
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.602
distinct devices was a logistical nightmare, so Facebook relied

6
00:00:20.602 --> 00:00:23.663
on an HTML5 mobile web app to deliver its

7
00:00:23.663 --> 00:00:24.335
newsfeed.

8
00:00:24.335 --> 00:00:26.947
The performance was painfully slow.

9
00:00:26.947 --> 00:00:29.410
Engineers attempted a compromise.

10
00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.516
They took their existing web application and wrapped it

11
00:00:33.516 --> 00:00:37.398
inside a native mobile container, a web view, hoping

12
00:00:37.398 --> 00:00:42.026
to extract native performance without rewriting the code base.

13
00:00:42.026 --> 00:00:45.758
The result remained sluggish and felt out of place

14
00:00:45.758 --> 00:00:46.952
on a smartphone.

15
00:00:46.952 --> 00:00:51.506
Development teams across the industry were caught between the

16
00:00:51.506 --> 00:00:55.536
speed of web development and the performance of native

17
00:00:55.536 --> 00:00:55.910
apps.

18
00:00:55.910 --> 00:01:00.463
During a 2013 Facebook hackathon, engineers Jordan Walke, who

19
00:01:00.463 --> 00:01:04.345
had already built React for the web, and Christopher

20
00:01:04.345 --> 00:01:09.570
Chedeau, started experimenting with a completely different approach to

21
00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.287
mobile user interfaces.

22
00:01:11.287 --> 00:01:15.318
They arrived at a structural insight: they could write

23
00:01:15.318 --> 00:01:20.095
the application's logic entirely in JavaScript, but instruct the

24
00:01:20.095 --> 00:01:24.350
device's native rendering layer to draw the actual visual

25
00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.022
elements.

26
00:01:25.022 --> 00:01:28.157
This experiment evolved into React Native.

27
00:01:28.157 --> 00:01:32.038
At the 2015 F8 conference, Facebook open sourced the

28
00:01:32.038 --> 00:01:37.189
framework, operating under a highly appealing philosophy: learn once,

29
00:01:37.189 --> 00:01:38.309
write anywhere.

30
00:01:38.309 --> 00:01:41.966
The engineering community adopted it immediately.

31
00:01:41.966 --> 00:01:46.445
Startups and tech giants alike integrated the framework into

32
00:01:46.445 --> 00:01:52.342
their production environments, with companies like Microsoft, Shopify, Discord,

33
00:01:52.342 --> 00:01:56.746
and Walmart building highly complex applications on the new

34
00:01:56.746 --> 00:01:57.269
system.

35
00:01:57.269 --> 00:02:01.897
By separating the underlying logic from the rendering process,

36
00:02:01.897 --> 00:02:06.151
React Native gave engineers the rapid iteration speeds of

37
00:02:06.151 --> 00:02:11.003
web development while outputting genuine native UI elements, like

38
00:02:11.003 --> 00:02:13.392
an actual UIButton on an iPhone.

39
00:02:13.392 --> 00:02:17.199
The engine that made this separation possible was a

40
00:02:17.199 --> 00:02:20.036
system known internally as the bridge.

41
00:02:20.036 --> 00:02:24.514
This diagram illustrates the mechanical flaw in that system.

42
00:02:24.514 --> 00:02:28.769
Every single interaction — drawing a pixel, responding to

43
00:02:28.769 --> 00:02:31.680
a swipe, or measuring a block of text —

44
00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.114
had to be serialized into a JSON text message,

45
00:02:35.114 --> 00:02:38.622
sent across a divide, and decoded by the native

46
00:02:38.622 --> 00:02:38.996
side.

47
00:02:38.996 --> 00:02:44.594
Because this communication was strictly asynchronous, it was mathematically

48
00:02:44.594 --> 00:02:47.953
impossible to call native code synchronously.

49
00:02:47.953 --> 00:02:51.088
Even a simple request to read the width of

50
00:02:51.088 --> 00:02:54.597
a layout box meant sending a message across the

51
00:02:54.597 --> 00:02:58.702
bridge and waiting for a callback, while other messages

52
00:02:58.702 --> 00:03:00.643
were piling up in a queue.

53
00:03:00.643 --> 00:03:05.346
As applications grew larger and user interfaces demanded higher

54
00:03:05.346 --> 00:03:09.824
frame rates, this original workaround devolved into a severe

55
00:03:09.824 --> 00:03:11.466
structural bottleneck.

56
00:03:11.466 --> 00:03:14.154
Users felt this bottleneck directly.

57
00:03:14.154 --> 00:03:20.499
High-frequency operations like complex screen transitions or scroll-driven animations

58
00:03:20.499 --> 00:03:24.529
would stutter and drop frames, imposing a hard ceiling

59
00:03:24.529 --> 00:03:26.545
on application performance.

60
00:03:26.545 --> 00:03:31.397
This legacy structure was also completely incompatible with newer

61
00:03:31.397 --> 00:03:32.666
web advancements.

62
00:03:32.666 --> 00:03:37.443
Features like React's concurrent mode, which allow the framework

63
00:03:37.443 --> 00:03:41.773
to pause and prioritize rendering tasks in the background,

64
00:03:41.773 --> 00:03:46.102
simply could not function over a slow asynchronous bridge.

65
00:03:46.102 --> 00:03:49.834
Rather than trying to patch the system again, Meta

66
00:03:49.834 --> 00:03:53.268
and Microsoft made a drastic decision in 2022.

67
00:03:53.268 --> 00:03:56.776
They chose to abandon the bridge and initiate a

68
00:03:56.776 --> 00:04:00.733
total rewrite of the framework's internal foundation.

69
00:04:00.733 --> 00:04:04.614
The engineering stakes were massive: they had to rip

70
00:04:04.614 --> 00:04:07.824
out and rebuild the core engine of the most

71
00:04:07.824 --> 00:04:12.153
popular mobile framework in the world, entirely behind the

72
00:04:12.153 --> 00:04:17.229
scenes, without breaking the millions of active applications relying

73
00:04:17.229 --> 00:04:17.677
on it.

74
00:04:17.677 --> 00:04:22.231
After years of incremental internal work, this rewrite, known

75
00:04:22.231 --> 00:04:26.411
as the New Architecture, shipped as the default standard

76
00:04:26.411 --> 00:04:29.770
in React Native version 0.76 in October 2024.

77
00:04:29.770 --> 00:04:32.756
This diagram outlines the new mechanics.

78
00:04:32.756 --> 00:04:36.712
The first pillar is JSI, or the JavaScript Interface.

79
00:04:36.712 --> 00:04:42.758
It eliminates JSON serialization completely, creating direct synchronous bindings

80
00:04:42.758 --> 00:04:45.819
between JavaScript and C++ memory spaces.

81
00:04:45.819 --> 00:04:47.909
The second pillar is Fabric.

82
00:04:47.909 --> 00:04:51.790
By bypassing the old bottleneck, this new shared C++

83
00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:57.463
rendering engine handles layout measurements synchronously and enables React

84
00:04:57.463 --> 00:05:02.092
to process UI rendering concurrently without freezing the main

85
00:05:02.092 --> 00:05:03.137
device thread.

86
00:05:03.137 --> 00:05:05.600
The third pillar is TurboModules.

87
00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:10.079
Instead of forcing an application to initialize every native

88
00:05:10.079 --> 00:05:15.080
feature, like Bluetooth or the camera, during startup, TurboModules

89
00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.663
lazily load these tools only at the exact moment

90
00:05:18.663 --> 00:05:22.843
they are requested by the JavaScript layer, cutting down

91
00:05:22.843 --> 00:05:23.888
startup times.

92
00:05:23.888 --> 00:05:27.770
These three pillars allow React Native to operate as

93
00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:31.502
a low-level engine, removing the need for a bridge

94
00:05:31.502 --> 00:05:35.682
and integrating the logic layer directly into the native

95
00:05:35.682 --> 00:05:36.279
runtime.

96
00:05:36.279 --> 00:05:40.982
Today, complex, gesture-driven animations run at 120 frames per

97
00:05:40.982 --> 00:05:43.445
second directly on the UI thread.

98
00:05:43.445 --> 00:05:47.625
The performance gap between React Native and pure native

99
00:05:47.625 --> 00:05:53.149
development is functionally negligible for most applications, supported by

100
00:05:53.149 --> 00:05:54.344
tools like Expo.

101
00:05:54.344 --> 00:05:57.852
The framework is now used by Shopify, Meta, and

102
00:05:57.852 --> 00:06:03.226
Microsoft to power their flagship applications, providing a standardized

103
00:06:03.226 --> 00:06:06.585
environment for production-scale mobile code.

104
00:06:06.585 --> 00:06:10.990
Ten years after the first prototype, the framework delivers

105
00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:15.095
the speed of web development alongside the execution of

106
00:06:15.095 --> 00:06:16.812
native mobile software.
